\section{Hamiltonian Derivation of Structural Invariants}

Let $H_{\text{TSSE}}$ denote the global TSSE Hamiltonian:
\[
H_{\text{TSSE}} = H_{\text{T}} + H_{\text{RPK}} + H_{\text{FK}}.
\]

Structural invariants arise from:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Hamiltonian symmetries.
  \item Temporal symmetry operators.
  \item Manifold constraints.
  \item Symplectic evolution.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{definition}[Hamiltonian Invariant]
A Hamiltonian invariant $\mathcal{I}_H$ satisfies:
\[
\{\mathcal{I}_H, H_{\text{TSSE}}\} = 0.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Hamiltonian Invariant Preservation]
If $\{\mathcal{I}_H, H_{\text{TSSE}}\} = 0$, then:
\[
\frac{d\mathcal{I}_H}{dt} = 0.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Hamilton’s equations yield:
\[
\frac{d\mathcal{I}_H}{dt} = \{\mathcal{I}_H, H_{\text{TSSE}}\}.
\]
If the Poisson bracket vanishes, the invariant is preserved.
\end{proof}

This lemma establishes the Hamiltonian foundation of TSSE invariants.

